{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ARCH.md | sed -n '1,110p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Architecture\n     2\t\n     3\tLiving architecture document. Update when you change architecture.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Three-role agent model\n     6\t\n     7\tDarkfactory uses three agent roles, each running as a separate AI conversation:\n     8\t\n     9\t**Orchestrator** -- the main Claude Code session. Plans the work, dispatches one worker at a time, runs validators, manages fix loops and circuit breakers. Uses plan mode to keep instructions alive across long conversations. Defined by `skills/mission/SKILL.md` (10 phases, 0-9).\n    10\t\n    11\t**Workers** -- each builds a single task from a brief. Default engine is Codex (via `darkfactory-dispatch`); Claude is an alternative. A worker receives only its brief, never the contract. Makes changes in one commit and writes a handoff report.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Validators** -- check each worker's output. Two run back-to-back per task: scrutiny (build, test, lint, code review, contract check, execution check, test health) and user-test (tests the feature as a user would). Validators see the contract and the code, never the proposal or design.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Information walls\n    16\t\n    17\tWorkers never see the contract. Validators never see the design.\n    18\t\n    19\tThis forces validation to test what the code *does*, not what someone *intended*. Fix workers receive symptom descriptions, never the contract, so they solve real problems instead of gaming a checklist.\n    20\t\n    21\t## File-backed state\n    22\t\n    23\tAll state lives on disk, not in memory. Sessions can crash and resume. Progress is visible from a second terminal.\n    24\t\n    25\t```\n    26\topenspec/changes/<change-name>/\n    27\t  proposal.md              # What and why\n    28\t  specs/<cap>/spec.md      # WHEN/THEN requirements\n    29\t  design.md                # Architecture decisions, module inventory, type catalog\n    30\t  tasks.md                 # Ordered checklist with topology metadata\n    31\t  coverage-audit.md        # PRD-to-task traceability\n    32\t  contract.md              # Observable behavior checks, traced to specs\n    33\t  mission/\n    34\t    config.yaml            # Engine per role (codex or claude)\n    35\t    progress.md            # Running log\n    36\t    briefs/task-N.md       # What each worker receives\n    37\t    handoffs/task-N.md     # What each worker reports\n    38\t    validations/           # Validator reports\n    39\t    issues/                # Failure details per attempt\n    40\t    logs/                  # Streaming output (tail -f)\n    41\t```\n    42\t\n    43\t## Skill structure\n    44\t\n    45\tSkills live in `skills/` at the repo root. `install.sh` symlinks each into `~/.claude/skills/` for Claude Code discovery.\n    46\t\n    47\tEach skill is a self-contained module. Public interface: frontmatter (name, description). Implementation: SKILL.md body. No skill imports from another at the filesystem level.\n    48\t\n    49\tThe mission skill is intentionally monolithic (~825 lines). Companion files are read on demand:\n    50\t- `templates/` -- artifact formats (plan, contract, worker brief, design, etc.)\n    51\t- `prompts/` -- instructions handed to validator and reviewer subagents\n    52\t\n    53\t12 skills total: mission, mission-resume, mission-status, mission-init, grill, language, prd, tdd, test-quality, security, adversarial, dispatch.\n    54\t\n    55\t## Build loop\n    56\t\n    57\tFor each task: dispatch worker, run scrutiny validator, run user-test validator. On failure: diagnostic triage, then fix worker with symptoms only (information wall maintained). Circuit breaker after 3 attempts (1 build + 2 fixes) -- pauses for human decision.\n    58\t\n    59\tAfter task 1 passes, a foundation audit checks module structure and type names against the design.\n    60\t\n    61\t## Planning constraints\n    62\t\n    63\tDesign phase scores 2-3 architecture candidates on a four-dimension rubric (separation of concerns, testability, task independence, module depth). A mandatory gate verifies the design includes an architecture decision, module inventory, type catalog, error strategy, integration points, and test strategy.\n    64\t\n    65\tBefore task generation, the orchestrator presents a decomposition strategy (layered, vertical, or hybrid) for human approval. Each task carries topology metadata: depends-on, consumes, produces, layers.\n    66\t\n    67\t## Configuration\n    68\t\n    69\tGlobal defaults in `~/.darkfactory/config.yaml`. Per-mission engine assignments in `mission/config.yaml` (worker, scrutiny_validator, usertest_validator, adversarial reviewers).\n    70\t\n    71\t## External dependencies\n    72\t\n    73\t- Claude Code (orchestrator, Claude workers, validators)\n    74\t- Codex CLI (Codex workers, adversarial reviewer)\n    75\t- darkfactory-dispatch (Rust supervisor: process isolation, timeout, heartbeat, structured results)\n    76\t- OpenSpec CLI (planning artifact generation)\n    77\t- GitHub CLI (PRD issues, draft PRs)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
