{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md | sed -n '84,124p'\"","aggregated_output":"    84\t\n    85\t## Planning determinism\n    86\t\n    87\t| Term | Definition | Aliases to avoid |\n    88\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    89\t| **Deterministic Anchors** | One-liner pointers in the plan to the six highest-leverage design decisions, positioned to survive context compaction | Anchors, plan summary, design cache |\n    90\t| **Architecture Candidate Scoring** | Evaluating 2-3 architecture options against a four-dimension rubric (separation of concerns, testability, task independence, module depth) to select an architecture deterministically | Architecture review, arch comparison |\n    91\t| **Task Independence** | A scoring dimension measuring how well an architecture allows tasks to be completed without cross-task coordination on conventions | Worker task-ability, decomposability |\n    92\t| **Task Topology** | Per-task metadata declaring which prior tasks it depends on, which surfaces it consumes, which surfaces it produces, and which architectural layers it touches | Task graph, dependency map |\n    93\t| **Foundation Audit** | A one-time check after task 1 passes validation, verifying that committed code matches the design's module inventory and type catalog for task 1's declared produces | Post-task-1 check, foundation check |\n    94\t| **Type Catalog** | A design.md section listing domain types with name, purpose, and owning module -- no field-level detail. Type names are drawn from this glossary when a matching term exists; types not in the glossary are named by the worker | Type registry, type list, types.md |\n    95\t| **Domain type** | A type that models the problem space. Excludes technical helpers (CLI parsers, test fixtures, generic containers) unless they are public compatibility surfaces | Helper type, utility type, internal type |\n    96\t| **Module Inventory** | A design.md section listing modules with interface width, what they hide, and why the boundary exists | Module table, module list |\n    97\t| **Design Template** | A reference document defining mandatory design.md sections and scoring rubrics, used by the orchestrator alongside openspec config rules | Design spec, design schema |\n    98\t| **Spec Reference** | A capability/scenario identifier (e.g., add-todo/1) used to trace contract assertions back to formal spec requirements | REQ-ID, requirement ID, spec ID |\n    99\t\n   100\t## Relationships\n   101\t\n   102\t- A **Mission** belongs to exactly one **Change**\n   103\t- A **Change** contains one **Proposal**, one **Design**, one **Tasks** list, one **Contract**, and one **Mission directory**\n   104\t- Each **Task** maps to one **Worker** invocation, one git commit, and one validation cycle\n   105\t- A **Contract** contains **Assertions** grouped by **Task**\n   106\t- The **Orchestrator** writes **Briefs** and reads **Handoffs**\n   107\t- The **Orchestrator** spawns Codex **Workers** through the **Dispatcher**, and Claude **Workers** and **Validators** through the Agent tool\n   108\t- The **Dispatcher** supervises the Codex process: it manages the **Process group**, emits **Heartbeats**, enforces the **Two-phase timeout**, and returns a **Dispatch result**\n   109\t- **Workers** read **Briefs** and write **Handoffs** -- they never see the **Contract**\n   110\t- **Validators** read the **Contract** and the commit diff -- they never see the **Proposal** or **Design**\n   111\t- A **Fix loop** produces versioned **Handoffs** (task-N-fix-v2.md) and re-runs both **Validators**\n   112\t- The **Circuit breaker** halts the **Fix loop** and returns control to the human\n   113\t- The **Dispatch store** is independent of the **Mission directory** -- it holds dispatcher metadata, not mission state\n   114\t- The **Scrutiny validator** runs deterministic checks (build, lint, **Silent skip** detection, **Diff coverage**, **Error handling smell** density, **File size pre-pass**) and LLM checks (code review, module depth, **Test quality review**)\n   115\t- **Diff coverage** measures added lines only -- a worker is responsible for covering the code they wrote, not backfilling pre-existing debt\n   116\t- The **Test quality review** (`/test-quality`) is an owned skill invoked by the **Scrutiny validator** as a subagent, following the same pattern as `/review` and `/simplify`\n   117\t- A **Tautological assertion** is worse than no test because it gives false confidence -- the **Scrutiny validator** treats it as a hard FAIL\n   118\t- **Deterministic Anchors** are populated from design.md during Phase 4.3 and stored in the **Plan** -- they reference the **Type Catalog**, **Module Inventory**, and **Architecture Candidate Scoring** results\n   119\t- The **Type Catalog** draws **Domain type** names from this glossary. **Worker briefs** list relevant **Type Catalog** entries as exact names to use in code. The **Foundation Audit** checks task 1 names against the **Type Catalog**. The module shape re-check verifies all **Type Catalog** names at the end\n   120\t- The **Foundation Audit** runs once after task 1, comparing committed code against the **Module Inventory** and **Type Catalog** from design.md\n   121\t- **Spec References** in the **Contract** trace back to specific **Spec** capability/scenario pairs (e.g., add-todo/1), replacing arbitrary row numbers\n   122\t- **Task Topology** metadata is written per task in tasks.md and reviewed during Phase 4.4 -- tasks touching more than 2 architectural layers are flagged for human review\n   123\t- The **Design Template** is a reference document in `templates/` -- the actual design.md generation is driven by openspec config rules that require the same sections\n   124\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
